Make GstMFDShowPinInfo::operator< const, so std::sort works under libc++.

UPSTREAM BUG, not a MinGW or a clang concern: a comparison operator that does not modify its
operand should be const, whatever standard library is in use.

gstmfcapturedshow.cpp sorts a std::vector<GstMFDShowPinInfo> with std::sort and no explicit
comparator, so the sort reaches for the type's own operator<. libc++'s default comparator,
std::__less<>, invokes it through const references:

    __algorithm/comp.h:42:18: error: invalid operands to binary expression
                              ('const GstMFDShowPinInfo' and 'const GstMFDShowPinInfo')
       42 |     return __lhs < __rhs;

A non-const member operator is not callable on a const object, so the instantiation fails.
libstdc++ happens to accept the same source because its sort instantiates paths in which the
left operand is not const -- which is luck, not correctness.

One word. Worth sending upstream: it costs nothing on GCC and unbreaks the plugin on clang.

--- a/subprojects/gst-plugins-bad/sys/mediafoundation/gstmfcapturedshow.cpp
+++ b/subprojects/gst-plugins-bad/sys/mediafoundation/gstmfcapturedshow.cpp
@@ -312,7 +312,7 @@
       gst_caps_unref (caps);
   }

-  bool operator< (const GstMFDShowPinInfo & other)
+  bool operator< (const GstMFDShowPinInfo & other) const
   {
     return gst_mf_source_object_caps_compare (caps, other.caps) < 0;
   }
